    "body": "DHL Global Forwarding has added six dedicated healthcare air routes connecting Europe and North America with pharmaceutical markets in Asia. Announced on Monday , the expansion is for temperature-controlled shipments , including medicines, vaccines and clinical trial materials.\nWhat’s Related\nThree routes connect Cincinnati with Singapore, Tokyo Narita and Seoul Incheon. The European routes connect Liège with Shanghai, Brussels with Shanghai, and Brussels with Singapore.\nDHL says the routes give pharmaceutical customers more shipping options and greater control over how sensitive cargo is handled. Most flights operate on DHL-branded aircraft, which the company says reduces handovers between transportation providers.\nThe additions follow the launch of DHL’s Brussels-Cincinnati healthcare route earlier this year.\nRELATED: DHL Adds Dedicated Brussels–Cincinnati Cold Chain Route\nProtecting cargo in flight and during transfers\nDHL uses Boeing 777 freighters with three cargo compartments that can be set to different temperatures. On the ground, thermal blankets and tested handling procedures are designed to limit exposure to temperatures outside a shipment’s required range.\nThe company’s approach combines controlled transportation with healthcare facilities and packaging selected for each shipment. DHL says it tests shipping routes and packaging against expected conditions, rather than relying on packaging alone to protect cargo throughout the trip.\nIts cold chain service also includes shipment monitoring and risk alerts through DHL LifeTrack. These tools let customers track shipments and receive alerts about potential problems during the flight.\nWhen DHL announced the Brussels-Cincinnati service in February, it said the wider operation connected more than 30 aviation hubs and handling locations that follow Good Distribution Practice standards for pharmaceutical shipments. DHL also said its temperature-controlled facilities and transportation could reduce reliance on heavy packaging and refrigerated air freight containers .\nSingapore facility is planned for late 2027\nThe new Singapore routes connect with a separate facility investment announced in September. DHL Global Forwarding plans to open its Singapore Coldchain Hub at Changi Airport in late 2027.\nThe $22 million facility will cover 3,047 square meters and include handling areas maintained at 2°C to 8°C and 15°C to 25°C. Direct access to the airport’s aircraft operating area is intended to limit temperature exposure as goods move between planes and the facility.\nDHL is also adding refrigerated operating and pallet -storage space at its healthcare facility in Indianapolis, about a 2.5-hour drive from its Cincinnati air hub.",
